Z||000000||Slapnutz2000||06-24-2000||05:36 PM||||I got a vavle job done on my 86 am4 about 1300 miles ago. I replaced the fuel filter, got a major tune up, and put a new head gasket put on...also replaced every major engine component.  My question is: How long should I expect this engine to last? I haven't had any problems so far (I know it's only been 1300 miles) and have seen a dramatic pickup in performance.  The car has 73,300 miles.<P>TIA,<BR>James<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Slapnutz2000||06-24-2000||05:41 PM||||Maybe I should learn to spell before I post?||||reg||11
Z||000002||Old Lar||06-24-2000||08:27 PM||||Some of the original 4 cylinder engines have 200000 miles on them.  The highest milage Fiero I've seen had 300000 miles on it.  It had three clutches, but the engine hadn't been apart.  It was a rather beat looking black coupe, that gets used, but well cared for.  Hard to say how many miles you'll get on the engine.  Too many variables to consider.  Change the oil frequently is the simplest thing to do.  Don't beat on the car.||||reg||1
Z||000003||286SE'S||06-26-2000||02:53 PM||||I always check the mileage of the Fieros I see at the junkyard. The highest mileage I have seen was 248,000 on a Indy pace car and the lowest has been 57,000 on an 84 SE. The average has been between 110,000 and 160,000. A total of 26 4 cylinder cars. The 6 cylinder cars always seem to have missing speedometers (I wonder why...grin!) I think that you will have at least 50,000 miles of good service if the car is well taken care of. I bought my 4cy SE in 1994 with...yes....72,000 miles. Daily Driver, it now has 112,000 and only needed a starter, ignition module and a Cat. There will be lots of other things that will cause you to break down outside the engine. Tires, brakes and struts will keep you busy. They are going to show their age eventually. Keep reading the forum and  catch those little things before they get serious. My 3 cents.<BR>Terry<p>[This message has been edited by 286SE'S (edited 06-26-2000).]||||reg||1
